nd that 'rate' has 0/1 replicas available. Then they tried to get pods labeled with 'app=rate' but found none. Now, they need to figure out why the deployment isn't creating pods.\n\nThe next logical step is to use the 'kubectl describe deployment' command. This command provides detailed information about the deployment, including events, conditions, and any errors that might be preventing the pods from being created. By examining the deployment's status, they can check for issues like image pull failures, resource limits, or configuration mistakes. \n\nThe user has already used 'kubectl get deployments' and 'kubectl get pods', so now using 'kubectl describe deployment rate' will give them deeper insights. The command will show the deployment's current state, the number of available replicas, any messages from the Kubernetes system, and events that might indicate why the pods aren't starting. This information is crucial for identifying the root cause, whether it's a problem with the container image, insufficient resources, or a misconfigured deployment spec.\n\nSo, the correct tool call here is to execute 'kubectl describe deployment rate -n hotel-reservation'. This will provide the necessary details to understand why the 'rate' deployment isn't creating pods, allowing the user to address the specific issue.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe deployment rate -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8a051e1f7a1a6e58', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 323, 'prompt_tokens': 3314, 'total_tokens': 3637, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb48-bd47-74b0-bc50-c7a77df8a3bb-0', tool_c