Set:   rate-6fff88f6f5 (1/1 replicas created)\nEvents:\n  Type    Reason             Age   From                   Message\n  ----    ------             ----  ----                   -------\n  Normal  ScalingReplicaSet  2m5s  deployment-controller  Scaled up replica set rate-6fff88f6f5 to 1\n', id='6c3aa51b-41e8-404b-97de-564293155806', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8a051e1f7a1a6e58'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='bec6801b-52db-470e-8d74-d520a2ff617e'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the \'rate\' deployment in the hotel-reservation namespace isn\'t available. From the previous steps, we know that the deployment has 0 available replicas, and the pod wasn\'t found when querying by the app label. The describe command shows that the deployment is using a replica set, but the pod is not running.\n\nLooking at the describe output, the conditions show "MinimumReplicasUnavailable," which suggests that the deployment couldn\'t reach the desired number of replicas. The event log mentions "Scaled up replica set rate-6fff88f6f5 to 1," but the pod isn\'t appearing. Maybe the pod is in a crash loop or has some issues preventing it from starting.\n\nNext steps: Check the logs of the pod to see if there are any errors. Since the pod might not be running, using kubectl logs on the pod\'s name could reveal issues. But first, we need the pod\'s name. Wait, earlier when we tried to get pods with label app=rate, there were no results. However, the deployment\'s replica set might have a pod with a different name. Alternatively, maybe the pod is in a terminated state. \n\nAlternatively, check the events in the deployment for more details. The event says "Scaled up replica set," but maybe there\'s an error in the pod\'s lifecycle. Another approach is to check the pod\'s status using kubectl describe pod 