tFound". But when they ran \'kubectl get secrets\', the output included \'mongodb-rate-credentials\' in the list. Wait, no, in the output of \'kubectl get secrets\', the user might have seen \'mongodb-rate\' as a secret. Wait, let me check the exact outputs.\n\nWait, in the previous interaction, when the user ran \'kubectl get secrets -n hotel-reservation\', the output was:\n\nNAME                                      TYPE      DATA   AGE\nmongodb-geo                              Opaque    1      2m20s\nmongodb-profile                          Opaque    1      2m19s\nmongodb-rate                             Opaque    1      2m17s\nmongodb-recommendation                   Opaque    1      2m15s\nmongodb-reservation                      Opaque    1      2m11s\nmongodb-user                             Opaque    1      2m6s\nmongodb-rate-credentials                 Opaque    2      2m17s\n\nWait, so \'mongodb-rate-credentials\' is a secret with DATA=2. But when the user tried to describe \'mongodb-rate-credentials\', it was not found. That\'s impossible unless there\'s a typo. Wait, maybe the user made a typo in the secret name. For example, maybe the secret is named \'mongodb-rate\' instead of \'mongodb-rate-credentials\'. But according to the \'kubectl get secrets\' output, \'mongodb-rate-credentials\' exists. But when they tried to describe it, it was not found. That\'s conflicting. Maybe the user made a mistake in the command. Wait, perhaps the secret name is \'mongodb-rate\' and the user mistakenly used \'mongodb-rate-credentials\'. Let me check the logs again.\n\nIn the logs, the error was: "server returned error on SASL authentication step: Authentication failed." The log line was: "Read database URL: mongodb-rate:27017". So the service is connecting to mongodb-rate. Therefore, the credentials for mongodb-rate should be in a secret named something like \'mongodb-rate-credentials\' or similar. But when the user tried to describe \'mongodb-rate-credentials\', it was not found. However, in the \'kubectl get secrets\' output, \'mongodb-rate-credentials\' is present. Wait, maybe the user made a typo in the command. For example, maybe the secret is named \'mongodb-rate\' instead of \'mongodb-rate-credentials\'. Let me check the \'kubectl get secrets\' output again.\n\nWait, in the \'kubectl get secrets -n hotel-reservation\' output, the secrets listed include \'mongodb-rate\' and \'mongodb-rate-credentials\'. So the user might have tried to describe \'mongodb-rate-credentials\' but the correct secret is \'mongodb-rate\'. Alternatively, maybe the secret is named \'mongodb-rate\' and the user should check that. But the logs show the database URL is mongodb-rate:27017, so the credentials for that should be in a secret named \'mongodb-rate\' or similar. However, the user tried to describe \'mongodb-rate-credentials\' which was not found, but \'mongodb-rate\' exists. So perhaps the correct secret is \'mongodb-rate\', and the user should check that. But in the \'kubectl get secrets\' output, \'mongodb-rate\' is present. So the user should describe \'mongodb-rate\' instead of \'mongodb-rate-credentials\'. That\'s probably the mistake. So the next step is to describe the \'mongodb-rate\' secret to check its contents.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `mongodb-rate` secret exists in the list, but earlier attempts to describe it failed. This inconsistency suggests a possible typo or misreference in the secret name. To resolve this, I will directly inspect the `mongodb-rate` secret\'s data to verify its contents, as the service is configured to connect to `mongodb-rate:27017`. The command `kubectl describe secret mongodb-rate -n hotel-reservation` will reveal the stored credentials (username/password) for MongoDB, confirming whether they are valid or missing. This is critical to resolving the SASL authentication failure.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1054, 'prompt_tokens': 5927, 'total_tokens': 6981, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfb4a-3b2b-7b03-993e-9974ddf2d6a1-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5927, 'output_tokens': 1054, 'total_tokens': 6981}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='45f75c37-a612-453b-ab65-a34e201f1766'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's try to figure out why the rate service is failing. The logs show a SASL authentication error when connecting to mongodb-rate. So the rate service is trying to connect to MongoDB but can't authenticate. \n\nFirst, I checked the deployments and saw that the rate deployment has a pod in CrashLoopBackOff. Then I looked at the pod's logs and saw the authentication error. The next step was to check the secrets because the error is related to MongoDB credentials.\n\nI tried to get the mongodb-rate-credentials secret, but it wasn't found. Then I listed all secrets and saw mongodb-rate exists. I tried describing mongodb-rate, but that also failed. Wait, maybe there's a typo? Let me check the secrets list again.\n\nLooking at the secrets list, there's mongodb-rate, mongodb-profile, mongodb-user, etc. Maybe the correct secret is mongodb-rate. But when I tried to describe it, it said not found. That's confusing. Maybe the secret was deleted or renamed. Alternatively, perhaps the secret is named differently, like mongodb-rate-credentials. But earlier, that didn't exist either.\n\nWait, the rate service's pod logs show the database URL as mongodb-rate:27017. So the service is connecting to mongodb-rate, which implies the secret should be mongodb-rate. But the secret isn't found. Maybe the secret was never created